WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:12.070
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Good morning ladies and gentlemen,
and welcome to the Board Meeting of NHS England.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:18.130
We are, as interested observers will have
already noted, in a different venue on this

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:24.780
occasion. We have moved from Southside in
Victoria Street, London, to Skipton House

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:30.420
here at the Elephant and Castle, which is
the area in which many of the staff working

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:35.090
for NHS England are based, as well as our
Leeds office and all of our regional offices

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:40.350
as well. A particular welcome here to our
home, to those members of the public who have

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:47.760
joined us in person this morning, and also
to those eagerly watching us online. It is

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:53.039
a great pleasure to welcome you to a meeting
which is true to the commitment that we have

00:00:53.039 --> 00:00:58.289
to openness and transparency. This is a meeting
in public; it is not a public meeting, but

00:00:58.289 --> 00:01:04.820
what I will assure those who are with us this
morning is that, at the close of the meeting,

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:09.450
our members will be happy to talk informally
to those of you who have taken the trouble

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:12.670
– which we much appreciate – to be with
us this morning.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:36.700
I 
start by saying that we have no apologies

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:43.350
for absence this morning; we have a full team.
I would invite members of the Board to review

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:49.200
the page in front of them relating to their
declarations of interest. Please, if there

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.891
are any changes since the last edition, would
you alert our Secretariat; but secondly, please

00:01:54.891 --> 00:02:00.690
could you just pause and consider whether
there are any potential conflicts of interest

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:06.909
arising between those which you have declared
or which otherwise exist, and the agenda that

00:02:06.909 --> 00:02:14.809
the Board is looking at today. I will take
that as a null return, thank you very much.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:20.770
We move on then to the formal business of
the Board, and we have the minutes of the

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:27.260
last meeting. They have been circulated. I
have not had any comments, but can I just

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:33.600
accept that they are regarded by you as a
fair and accurate record? Thank you. I have

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:41.030
had no matters arising from the minutes. Can
I just check that that remains the case? Good,

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:43.459
thank you very much.

00:02:43.459 --> 00:02:50.430
May we move on to the Chairman’s report,
for me just to report on three things. First,

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:57.490
to report formally to the Board the appointment
of a new Chair of our Audit and Risk Assurance

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:02.020
Committee, Joanne Shaw. I am delighted that
she is with us today but has not yet formally

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:08.900
taken up her role on the Board, which will
commence on 1st October. I know the Board

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:14.610
shares my view that we have made an outstanding
appointment of somebody who has great experience,

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:20.209
both within the NHS and with the National
Audit Office, and will bring a fresh intelligence

00:03:20.209 --> 00:03:26.660
to our deliberations. We are delighted to
be able to welcome Joanne informally to our

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:28.240
meeting this morning.

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:35.120
Secondly, just for the public record, to observe
that today’s meeting is part of a cycle

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:40.849
of meetings. On Tuesday, we had the Specialised
Commissioning Committee, chaired by Noel Gordon.

00:03:40.849 --> 00:03:47.110
We will have a report from that committee
later on the agenda today. Yesterday was the

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:51.540
Commissioning Committee, which looks at the
heartland, I suppose, of what we actually

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:59.750
do as an organisation, and we will have a
report from that later today also, with David

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:04.040
Roberts as the Chairman. The Commissioning
Committee was looking at a number of issues

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:12.040
around urgent and emergency care; about planning,
financial planning, particularly for 2017/2018

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:19.040
and beyond; and also about building future
commissioning capacity. It is that development

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:24.030
that occurs in these committees that helps
the Board to function, not only in these formal

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:28.820
meetings where we take formal decisions, but
also in ensuring that we have a proper line

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:34.400
of sight and a proper line of governance through
the organisation for all of our activities.

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:40.190
Then yesterday we had, as always, a development
session in which all members of the Board,

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:45.430
sleeves rolled up literally, around the table
struggling with some of the huge strategic

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:50.780
issues that are coming through the system,
some of which we will be referring again to

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:55.620
today in the course of the agenda, in particular
the state of play with sustainability and

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:58.890
transformational planning across England.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:06.260
The third item on the Chairman’s report
is the consultation paper that was launched

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:11.060
last week on conflicts of interest across
the NHS. Members of the Board will recall

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:19.150
that in June you authorised me to chair a
pan-NHS task and finish group on conflicts

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:24.600
of interest. I have to say that I was very
pleased that we were able to attract onto

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:30.770
that group representation not only from the
other arm’s-length bodies of the NHS and

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:36.760
the Department of Health, but also critical
partners for this endeavour, including the

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:43.169
BMA; the British Medical Journal, which is
separate from the BMA; the Association of

00:05:43.169 --> 00:05:48.479
British Pharmaceutical Industries; the Association
of British Healthcare Industries; the General

00:05:48.479 --> 00:05:56.370
Medical Council; etc. We were able to get
a group of representatives of different areas

00:05:56.370 --> 00:06:03.410
that I think were combined in their vision
that what was going on at the moment was fragmented,

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:10.350
disjointed and inconsistent, and that we needed
to be able to pursue a clear vision and set

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:17.100
of principles about where the NHS pound was
being invested across the entire Health Service

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:23.720
and beyond, to try to ensure that it always
hit its mark and it was not deflected by conflicts

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:29.610
of interest held by those who were taking
decisions regarding our expenditure.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:35.520
We published a consultation report last week,
and I think the headlines of that are first

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:42.930
of all, how we could take best experience
from all parts of the NHS, and also other

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:48.800
sectors who had been able to deal – in the
commercial sector and the voluntary sector

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:54.520
– with conflicts of interest.
Secondly, to understand what interests needed

00:06:54.520 --> 00:07:00.610
to be declared and, having been declared,
how they could be handled to avoid a potential

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:03.250
conflict of interest becoming an actual conflict
of interest.

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:11.040
Thirdly, we are conscious that in the changing
world of the NHS, in which we are asking people

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:17.320
to move out of traditional silos and to move
into much more cross-working across different

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:24.210
institutions – as we will see, characterised
particularly with the STP process, but also

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:30.680
as we move into new models of care when we
are asking GPs to work in different ways and

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:39.130
to undertake strategy procurement and planning
and implementation in ways in which there

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:44.410
are potentially a number of conflicts of interest
tripwires – that we have a clear set of

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:51.410
principles that we can apply to assist them.
Because this is a process which we understand

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:58.740
works both ways. It works in the sense of
protecting public expenditure and ensuring

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:05.080
that it is not distorted by conflicts of interest,
but secondly, to protect those who are taking

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:09.919
the decisions by giving them a clear set of
protocols which can be followed, and which

00:08:09.919 --> 00:08:16.650
will ensure that there can be no suspicion
of false motives being deployed in the way

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:21.620
in which they take the decisions.
Then finally, around private practice, which

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:27.830
is probably the area which has attracted more
press publicity than the remaining areas.

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:34.419
On this, the panel were very clear that there
was an area which needed to be assured that

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:42.529
private practice would not be in conflict
with the NHS duties of those involved in private

00:08:42.529 --> 00:08:48.750
practice. It is a complex area. Our proposals
will not be uncontroversial, but our request

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:55.350
is, as across the whole of the consultation
process, that those who have ideas on how

00:08:55.350 --> 00:09:00.630
we could improve our approach, please let
us hear them. We will be coming back to the

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:05.490
topic at the end of the consultation period,
which is towards the end of October, and in

00:09:05.490 --> 00:09:10.470
November considering all the responses we
have had, while we then proceed to build up

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:16.779
a final document which we will be proposing
to issue as statutory guidance across the

00:09:16.779 --> 00:09:21.859
whole of the NHS.
Thank you. I have no further matters in the

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:26.800
Chairman’s report. I am happy to invite
any comments, observations or queries from

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:27.840
the Board on that.

